[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says the date reports are one issue with one fix queued. The reports
say otherwise. They were merged on subject line alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)),
and "wrong date" is a subject that more than one bug produces.

<Callout kind="warn">
The fix queued for #4471 is the parser fix. It closes the <code>parser</code> problem only. The other
problems below stay open after it ships.
</Callout>

## The problems in #4471

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

Each row has its own ticket and its own count. The table is built from the ticket export at render
time, so the counts follow the queue.

## How a report is placed

1. Count each report once: the export pages overlap, so the same id can appear twice.
2. If the desk read it as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`), it is `one-day`.
3. Otherwise ask the tenant, not the reporter's browser: a day-first tenant on a `booking-v3`
   form gets `hint`; every other day-first report is `parser` (`causes.ts`).

Intake's tag is not used. It is a guess from the reporter's browser and often names the wrong cause:
compare the "tagged as" column on [#4471](/kb/incidents/4471).

## What each problem is

**`parser`** — the shared parser reads every date month first. Day-first tenants get day and month
swapped: 7 April becomes 4 July.

**`hint`** — `booking-v3` prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type month first, as told,
and the form reads the value the tenant's way. Same symptom as `parser`, different code, different fix.

**`one-day`** — the date moves by one day, not by a swap: Tuesday becomes Monday, a client in Lisbon
and a client in Toronto see different days for the same meeting, an invite arrives before the
booking. That is time-zone handling, not parsing.

## For the desk

- Merge into a ticket only reports with the same problem, not the same subject.
- When a new date report comes in, place it with the steps above and add it to that problem's ticket.
- Do not close #4471's reports when the parser fix ships; check `hint` and `one-day` first.

